  "notes": "One secondary caption gives an April 2024 unveiling date; Commons photos of N8719Q from Jan/Apr 2025 are titled without livery, and Southwest's April 2026 release calls it newly introduced. 2026 favored. Drawn from 2026 photos of N8719Q. The stripes' ripples are drawn as an even wave: the real folds (with gray shading on the white) vary along the fuselage. No 'Liberty One' lettering was seen by the cockpit. Re-checked 2026-11-15: the fin is the state jets' Heart fin as re-measured on Colorado One (the same fin on every 737), copied with its 'Southwest' title and flag. The rippling stripes, traced as points along their waves, made smooth. No 'Liberty One' lettering is legible on either photo (the America250 mark is by the cockpit), so no fuselage title. Drawn on the 737-8. 2026-11-15: a Commons photo whose source is airliners.net was dropped (the dataset doesn't draw from photo sites); its parts now cite the other photo. 2026-11-23: the fin is now Independence One's (navy, white and red on the Heart's lines, white 'Southwest', the small flag), copied from SWA/independence-one with its palette (its navy as finNavy). The swap rests on dated spotter photos in the Infinite Flight forum's Southwest livery thread, evidence only: nothing is measured from them, and Southwest hasn't announced it; no Commons photo since. The fin's geometry is Independence One's measured fin, drawn on a 737 MAX 8; fin space is shares of the fin's height and chord, and the Heart's lines it uses are the same on every 737 (re-measured on Colorado One), so it carries to the 737-800. The dorsal fillet is drawn navy with the fin (assumed, low). 2026-11-23: engine cowlings measured off the right side (N8719Q, 2026 press photo): navy from the inlet back to a diagonal edge (at the top about a fifth of the way back, reaching the bottom near the cowl's end), then a red stripe over the top, white, and a red stripe low and aft; the cowl's paint ends at x 0.89 (the type's cowl end), bare metal nozzle behind. No star on the cowling in any photo (the star by the inlet is the fuselage's). One side measured: the other drawn the same."
